::ALG2_U01_L08_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L08_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x) = 2x<sup>2</sup> - 3, what is f(4)? {
=29 #Correct. 2(4 squared) - 3 = 32 - 3 = 29.
~13 #This substitutes 4 but does not square it before multiplying.
~32 #This omits subtracting 3.
~5 #This uses 2 times 4 minus 3, not 2 times 4 squared minus 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L08_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L08_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (x + 5)(x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 7x - 10 #This adds the outer and inner coefficients incorrectly.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 10 #Correct. x squared minus 2x plus 5x minus 10 combines to x squared plus 3x minus 10.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 10 #The middle term should be positive 3x.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 10 #This omits the middle terms and has the wrong constant operation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L08_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L08_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 25. {
=(x - 5)(x + 5) #Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~(x - 25)(x + 1) #This does not multiply back to x squared minus 25.
~(x - 5)(x - 5) #This expands to x squared minus 10x plus 25.
~x(x - 25) #This expands to x squared minus 25x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L08_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L08_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 16 = 0. {
~x = -4 and x = 4 #These solve x squared minus 16 equals 0.
=x = -4i and x = 4i #Correct. x squared equals -16, so x equals plus or minus 4i.
~x = -16i and x = 16i #This uses 16 instead of the square root of 16.
~x = 0 and x = 16 #These do not satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L08_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L08_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3; MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 9x = 0. {
~x = -3 and x = 3 #This leaves out the solution x = 0.
=x = -3, 0, 3 #Correct. Factor x(x squared - 9), then x(x - 3)(x + 3) = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 9 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 3.
~x = -9, 0, 9 #These use the coefficient 9 instead of its square root.
}
